apy has proven clinical  efficacy and tremendous potential in  multiple cancers but even patients who  experience clinical benefits from these  new agents often achieve only partial  responses such that additional work is  necessary to improve immunotherapeutic  strategies  as this figure illustrates solid tumors  are complex tissues not only are they  comprised of proliferating tumor cells  but are supported by a plethora of  thermal cells that participate in  functions such as tissue remodeling and  new antigenesis including many different  immune cells  all these cell populations are in  constant communication and influence  each other's activity function and fate  it's becoming increasingly clear that a  comprehensive analysis of the tumor  microenvironment is key to understand  the dynamics of  the anti-tumor immune response  in our case we're interested in  phenotyping tumor cells fibroblasts  endothelial cells t cells b cells and  myeloid cells  and sequencing tumor cell mutations  identifying new antigens in tumor  specific t and b cell clones  however  to do this we are dependent on the  availability of tumor material  this brings us to the challenges  routinely faced when planning and  performing experiments in cancer  research  most of the tumor samples from patients  we get weigh between 0.4 and 1 gram  after dissociation  we get between 4 and 20 million live  cells  it turns out that the re the frequency  of tills or tumor-infiltrating  leukocytes in the tumor can be very  reduced as illustrated by this graph on  the left  i calculated in this table on the right  the absolute number of t b and myeloid  cells that we would obtain from a tumor  with 20 million live cells containing  the average frequency of observed  we that we have observed  among our samples  we'd get 1 million t-cells 200 000 b  cells in 1.8 million myeloid cells  however  if we have to divide our dissociated  tissue in multiple samples to  individually analyze the different cell  populations we would  waste the majority of ourselves  to prevent that we develop tools and  workflows that allow extracting the  maximum amount of information from  limited material while minimizing the  experimental time and work for in  workload  i will start by briefly summarizing  examples of our strategy and then zoom  in on specific portions of it  it all starts with the resection or  biopsy of the tumor sample  the tissue is stored and transported in  tissue storage solution  upon arrival a small tissue section is  collected for future immunochemistry  analysis  the remainder is dissociated and we take  a small sample to identify and quantify  the immune cell populations  we can then isolate  t cells  using halle cd4 cdh till microbead kits  these new reagents can be released from  the cell surface after positive  selection of the cells of interest  the isolated t cells can then be used  for phenotypical analysis  undergo further magnetic removal of dead  cell  in order to obtain cells with high  viability which is required for  downstream applications such as single  cell immune profiling and rna sequencing  will even be used for example for  further magnetic subset enrichment  followed by in vitro expansion and  further analysis  the negative friction from the t-cell  isolation can also be used  the  realise cd45 till micro beads kits  can be used to isolate the rest of the  tumor infiltrating leukocytes  after magnetic release these cells can  be further used to isolate for example  myeloid cells or b cells which can then  be used for downstream applications such  as phenotype culture or sequencing  the negative fraction from the cd45  isolation can also be used for tumor  cell enrichment  this is performed using a negative  selection kit and the tumor cells can  then be used for multiple downstream  applications such as next generation  sequencing a new epitope prediction  phenotype culture or establishment of  patient-derived xenograft models  finally  the 