ficient  dissociation  this means the disruption of  extracellular matrix in cell adhesion  components without destroying the  integrity of the cell membrane while  simultaneously preserving the cell  surface epitopes  the interest of optimized enzymatic  dissociation can be showcased in this  example just doing a mechanical  dissociation will result in the loss of  cell populations which are closely  attached to the tumor cells such as  tumor reactive t cells  using the tumor association kits will  enable the identification of these rare  cell populations  furthermore we do in-house control of  the quality of the enzymes used and the  tumor dissociation kit was optimized for  epitope preservation we tested over 200  epitopes the vast majority of which was  not affected by our enzyme composition  i'm not expecting you to be able to read  this but this list of tested epitopes  can be accessed on our website  after dissociation we took a small  sample to identify and quantify the  immune cell populations using our age  color immunophenotyping kit  here i am showing the plots with the  gating strategy  after excluding dead cells and doublets  we gauge on cd45 positive leukocytes  from those we use a morphology gate to  exclude small debris in large tumor  cells we can then identify cd14 positive  macrophages and from the cd14 low  population we plot cd19 versus cd3 to  identify b cells  from the non-b cell gate  we plot cd16 cd56 in the same channel  against side scatter to identify  neutrophils and eosinophils  from the site scatter low gate we plot  cd16 in cd5 t6 against cd3 to identify  in k cells t cells and cd3 positive cd56  positive cells  finally from the t cell gate we plot cd4  versus cd8 to identify our major t cell  populations  we can then quantify the frequency of  the major leukocyte populations in the  tumor and it becomes quite obvious that  the frequencies of each of the  individual populations is quite low  because these cells are so often found  in low frequencies we develop new  reagents for the magnetic isolation of t  cells specifically from the tumor  based on the new release technology  briefly  the release technology relies on  recombine recombinantly engineered low  affinity antibody fragments that are  multimeterized in the realist complex  cells are labeled with these  biotinylated complexes in anti-biotin  microbeads  the target cells are then retained on a  column placed on a magnet  while non-target cells flow through  the target cells are then eluded and the  microbeads are removed resulting in  beet-free cells that can be used for  second magnetic labeling in isolation  for certain applications the release  complex can also be removed resulting in  label-free cells  using these new reagents we were  consistently able to quickly isolate  tumor infiltrating leukocytes with high  purity and directly use them for  analysis or because of the additional  flexibility provided by the hayali  system we have a world of possibilities  for isolation of specific populations  we can purify t-rex from tumors  combining really cd4-2 with ca25  microbeads we can generate high  viability tills for rna-seq using dead  cell removal kit after t cell isolation  we can isolate tumor-resident memory t  cells isolate tumor-specific versus  bystander cells we can isolate untouched  gamma delta t cells and so on  we isolated t cells from our dissociated  ovarian carcinoma sample using reality  cd4 cd8 still microbead kit  in addition to this  we also have available really cd4 cd8  and cd3 micro beat kits for tumors t  cell  and we will soon also have the realist  cd45 till microbead kit available  using these brand new reagents we were  able to quickly isolate tills with high  purities  on the top row you can see the frequency  of cd3 positive cells among live single  lymphocyte-sized  cells in the bulk sample as compared to  after isolation on the bottom row  we were able to achieve over 92 percent  purity of tumor infiltrating t cells  from our ovarian carcinoma sample  after magnetic enrichment of our cells  of interest we are now ready for  analysis  this is done  using affinity antibodies and analyzed  on the max quant a fully automated flow  cytometer  the affinity antibodies are genetically  engineered to enhance reproducibility  and eliminate bias  all antibodies have one universal igg-1  isotype which reduces the complexity of  the experimental planning and a mutated  fc region that eliminates seo receptor  binding mediated background  using magnetically isolated t cells from  the ovarian tumor sample greatly  facilitated  flow cytometry analysis  having highly enriched cells of interest  reduces the number of total events we  need to acquire for meaningful analysis  of rare subpopulations resulting in  shorter acquisition time reduced  background and cleaner populations  in this example we interrogated the  expression of pd1 cd39 tim three and  four one bb  among cd4 t cells on the top row and  among city h t cells on the bottom row  in addition we were interested in  performing rna-seq into immune profiling  t and b cells infiltrating this tumor  sample  require preserved analect of the  dissociated tumor  and at a later time point we thought our  sample and remove the dead cells using  the dead cell removal kit  we then used the really cd4 cdh-2  microbeads to isolate t cells for single  cell tcr sequencing in rna-seq  from the negative fraction we isolated b  cells in plasma cells for single cell  bcr sequencing  this way we could use the majority of t  cells for the t cell analysis and the  majority of b cells for b cell analysis  we use the 10x platform which can  generate up to 10 000 single cell gems  per sample  so a direct effect of magnetically  isolating the cells of interest is that  we greatly increase the frequency of  single cell tcrs in bcrs on our sample  as you can see in this table  in these graphs i am displaying in the  x-axis the 50 or 25 more represented  clonotypes in t or b cells respectively  and in the y-axis the number of cells  that express that particular clonotype  in the isolated sample  as you can see we were able to identify  several chronotypes that were  represented multiple times in the  isolated samples  in contrast  several of those chronotypes would have  gone unnoticed when we analyzed the bulk  samples for example a clonal type that  was present in 28 t cells in the  isolated sample was only present in  three cells in the bulk sample  on the b cells we were able to identify  one chronotype represented 120 cells  from magnetically isolated b cells but  was only found in four cells in the bulk  sample  these results highlight the degree to  which magnetic isolation can improve the  resolution of single cell rna-seq and  immune profiling  with this workflow we were able to  identify and quantify tumor-infiltrating  immune populations  isolate multiple cell populations for  downstream analysis  characterize phenotypically the isolated  populations and increase the resolution  of single cell rna-seq and immune  profiling of cells of interest  however in order to completely  characterize the tumor microenvironment  it may be useful to access the spatial  distribution of different cell  populations in their phenotypes  in order to do that  before tumor dissociation we collected a  small tissue section and used our maxima  platform which allows the staining of  hundreds of markers in a single sample  it uses the principle of an iterative  staining process conducted by the maxima  imaging platform in a completely  automated manner  briefly this platform stains the sample  with the first specificity and acquires  a fluorescence microscope image  the staining is then erased  the sample is stained with a second  specificity and the second image is  collected  this cycle can be repeated hundreds of  times in a fully automated way  as an example  i am showing a section from our initial  ovarian cancer  sample  with a region rich in tumor ios in the  top right corner  characterized by expression of afcam and  t-span h  in a stronghold region without tumor  cells  in the following slides we will keep  epcamp staining in red tried entertained  tumor cells and the indicated marker  will be displayed in white  looking at cd45 expression we can  appreciate that immune cells tend to  accumulate outside of the tumor area  we can locate the macrophages using cd14  expression for example  td-30h identifies b cells  plasma cells can be identified by cd  138 expression  pd3 expression identifies t cells  of which the majority are cd4 cells  consistent with our flow cytometry data  but also we can find our cd8 t cells  and we can further characterize  phenotypically the t cell  in this case with the expression of the  costume  co-stimulatory molecule cd27  or cd28  41bb  icos  but also markers that correlates with  tissue resident memory t cells like cd69  and cd103  expression of chemokine receptors can  also be assessed like cxcl cxcr3  we can identify cells that express pdl1  for negatively regulating t selectivity  and the expression of immune checkpoints  like ctla4  pd1  and tim3  we can even identify which cells were  actively  proliferating using t67 staining  in this case unfortunately this  indicates that tumor cells are the ones  proliferating in this sample  in summary i showed you here today new  workflows that allow the complete  characterization of the tumor  microenvironment  these start with tissue preservation  during shipping with tissue storage  solution  automated tissue dissociation with  optimized enzymatic mix  in identification and quantification of  tumor infiltrating immune populations  by implementing magnetic enrichment  steps we are able to  analyze multiple distinct populations  while minimizing the loss of limited  material and enhance the phenotypic  characterization of the isolated cell  populations  using the new realist technology we can  perform consecutive enrichment of  dedicated  subpopulations  and have complete flexibility for  studying several cell populations from  just one sample  in addition magnetic enrichment results  in increased  resolution of single cell rna-seq and  immune  profiling of cells of interest  finally  with maximum imaging platform we can  study the spatial distribution of  different self-populations in their  phenotypes within the tumor  microenvironment  using these workflows it becomes  feasible to fully embrace the complexity  of the tumor microenvironment  i would like to acknowledge my team and  all my colleagues at milton biotech that  made it possible to put together these  workflows as well as our collaborators  at the university of guttingham bonn and  cologne for providing material and  testing our workflows offsite  thank you dr evaristo for your  informative presentation and for your  time today  we would also like to thank labruz and  our sponsor miltoni biotech for  underwriting today's educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  all questions submitted today and during  the on-demand period will be addressed  by our speaker via the contact  information you provided at the time of  registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand  labyrints will alert you via email when  it's available for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event  until next time goodbye

